WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.854
Picture two kitchens, each with four chopping boards.

2
00:00:05.854 --> 00:00:08.711
In the first, all the work is chopping, all day.

3
00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:11.329
In the second, every dish goes into an oven.

4
00:00:11.329 --> 00:00:16.209
A cook spends one minute getting it ready, then waits nine minutes while it bakes.

5
00:00:16.459 --> 00:00:19.027
How many cooks should each kitchen hire?

6
00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:27.052
Every thread pool asks the same question, and the answer is often habit: ten because it sounds right, two hundred to be safe.

7
00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:30.005
So here's the question we rarely ask out loud.

8
00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:31.931
Are more threads always safer?

9
00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:36.406
The first kitchen needs four.

10
00:00:36.406 --> 00:00:40.680
A fifth cook would only stand around holding a knife, waiting for a board.

11
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.570
The second kitchen needs about forty, because a cook waiting for the oven doesn't need a board at all.

12
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.647
Let's map it.

13
00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.046
The cooks are threads.

14
00:00:49.046 --> 00:00:54.389
The boards are cores, the parts of the processor that each run one thread at a time.

15
00:00:54.389 --> 00:01:00.177
And chopping is work that keeps a core busy from start to finish, with nothing to wait for.

16
00:01:00.177 --> 00:01:02.021
That's called CPU-bound work.

17
00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:12.654
The lesson's demo tests the chopping kitchen: 32 checksums, short fingerprints of data, with no waiting at all, on 4 cores.

18
00:01:12.654 --> 00:01:15.524
One thread takes 359 milliseconds.

19
00:01:15.524 --> 00:01:16.284
Two: 171.

20
00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:17.044
Four: 92.

21
00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:18.817
What will eight take?

22
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:21.881
96.

23
00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:22.763
No faster.

24
00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:23.820
Sixteen: 98.

25
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.814
And the checksum was the same on every row, so it was the same work.

26
00:01:30.064 --> 00:01:32.256
A core runs one thread at a time.

27
00:01:32.256 --> 00:01:39.497
So a fifth thread gets a core only by taking it from another one, and the switch costs time and adds no work.

28
00:01:39.497 --> 00:01:42.088
That's the fifth cook, holding a knife.

29
00:01:42.088 --> 00:01:48.267
So for CPU-bound work, use about one thread per core, the number availableProcessors returns.

30
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:51.700
Now the oven kitchen, where most programs live.

31
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.576
To build one product page, our shop's program works for 20 milliseconds.

32
00:01:56.576 --> 00:02:05.177
Then it asks a slow web service, a program on another computer, for the stock level, and waits 180 milliseconds for the answer.

33
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:10.542
Work that mostly waits like this is called I/O-bound, after input and output.

34
00:02:10.542 --> 00:02:16.256
And a waiting thread uses no core, just as a cook waiting for the oven needs no board.

35
00:02:16.506 --> 00:02:20.715
So with one core, how many threads does it take to keep that core busy?

36
00:02:23.515 --> 00:02:24.196
About ten.

37
00:02:24.196 --> 00:02:31.274
Each page needs the core for 20 milliseconds out of every 200, so while one thread works, nine can wait.

38
00:02:31.274 --> 00:02:34.949
With four cores, that's forty: the oven kitchen again.

39
00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:47.483
Brian Goetz's book, Java Concurrency in Practice, writes it as a formula: cores, times the target CPU use, times one plus the wait over the compute.

40
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:52.961
On 8 cores at 80 percent, that's 8 times 0.8 times 10: 64 threads.

41
00:02:53.211 --> 00:02:56.466
Here the kitchen picture breaks, and not in our favor.

42
00:02:56.466 --> 00:02:59.540
A real cook would chop onions while the dish bakes.

43
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.578
A Java thread can't: it waits for its answer and does nothing else.

44
00:03:03.578 --> 00:03:07.315
That's why we need ten threads where one clever cook would do.

45
00:03:07.315 --> 00:03:09.485
So measure the wait and the compute.

46
00:03:09.485 --> 00:03:10.510
Don't guess them.

47
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.131
The formula starts from the processor.

48
00:03:13.131 --> 00:03:18.560
A busy shop asks from the other side: how many requests are inside our program at once?

49
00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.046
Picture the ticket window at a cinema.

50
00:03:21.046 --> 00:03:26.047
About 2 people join the line every minute, and each one spends about 5 minutes there.

51
00:03:26.047 --> 00:03:29.283
How many people do you see at the window at any moment?

52
00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:32.812
About 10.

53
00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:35.403
Two a minute, each staying five.

54
00:03:35.653 --> 00:03:37.975
That multiplication is Little's law.

55
00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:42.683
The number inside equals the arrival rate, times the time each one stays.

56
00:03:42.683 --> 00:03:46.681
It holds for a ticket window, a supermarket, or a thread pool.

57
00:03:46.931 --> 00:03:54.451
Our shop's website gets 400 requests a second at its busiest, and each one takes 250 milliseconds.

58
00:03:54.451 --> 00:03:59.132
400 times a quarter of a second: 100 requests inside at once.

59
00:03:59.132 --> 00:04:02.738
If each one holds a thread, that's 100 threads.

60
00:04:02.988 --> 00:04:09.245
Turned around, threads divided by the time each one is held is the most a pool can finish.

61
00:04:09.245 --> 00:04:13.138
50 threads at a quarter of a second finish 200 a second.

62
00:04:13.138 --> 00:04:17.170
If 400 arrive, the other 200 a second pile up on the rail.

63
00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:21.063
That's last lesson's rail with no end, now with numbers.

64
00:04:21.313 --> 00:04:23.976
Does it hold on a real machine?

65
00:04:23.976 --> 00:04:32.654
The demo sends 200 requests, each waiting 50 milliseconds, through pools of 5, 10, 20 and 40 threads.

66
00:04:32.654 --> 00:04:36.778
The law predicts 100, 200, 400 and 800 a second.

67
00:04:37.028 --> 00:04:39.772
Measured: 96, 181, 389 and 689.

68
00:04:39.772 --> 00:04:43.754
The first three land within about 10 percent.

69
00:04:43.754 --> 00:04:51.277
With 40 threads, 200 requests are only five rounds, so starting the pool weighs more.

70
00:04:51.472 --> 00:04:55.047
One honest limit: a request needs more than a thread.

71
00:04:55.047 --> 00:05:00.578
Give the oven kitchen only two ovens, and extra cooks just stand in line for them.

72
00:05:00.578 --> 00:05:06.446
In the demo, with only 10 database connections, the rows go flat at about 200 a second.

73
00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:08.985
So, are more threads always safer?

74
00:05:08.985 --> 00:05:09.195
No.

75
00:05:09.195 --> 00:05:11.084
For chopping, one per core.

76
00:05:11.084 --> 00:05:14.724
For waiting, cores times one plus wait over compute.

77
00:05:14.724 --> 00:05:18.223
To count requests, arrival rate times time inside.

78
00:05:18.223 --> 00:05:22.282
More threads help only while threads are the scarce thing.

79
00:05:22.472 --> 00:05:23.311
Quick check.

80
00:05:23.311 --> 00:05:26.948
20 threads, and each request takes 100 milliseconds.

81
00:05:26.948 --> 00:05:30.236
How many requests a second can the pool finish?
